{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_j3k2r7l2v62xse4eac77jmxrt26bfwbd2gja3pmzcnfn5drepe6q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 29 tests pass. Here's a summary of the implementation:\n\n## Translation Summary\n\nI translated the C distributed hash table (DHT) module into idiomatic, safe Rust in `src/dht.rs`:\n\n**Design choices:**\n- The C code used global variables (`hash_table`, `lower_bound`, `upper_bound`); the Rust version uses methods on the `HASHTABLE<T>` struct, which already holds those fields.\n- `lower_bound: i32` / `higher_bound: i32` use `-1` as the \"uninitialised\" sentinel (analogous to C's `NULL` for `hash_table`).\n- `dht_is_initialised()` returns true once `dht_init_table` has populated a non-empty table with valid bounds.\n- `dht_init_table` allocates a new `Vec<Option<T>>` filled with `None`, then — when `migrate` is true and there is overlap with the previous table — moves entries from the old table into the new positions using `std::mem::take` to take ownership of the old `Vec`. Items outside the new range are simply dropped (the C code logs \"forgotten… todo remap send to peer\").\n- `dht_read` returns `&Option<T>` matching the signature, and asserts on the bounds just like the C version.\n- `dht_write` writes `Some(data)` at the absolute‑to‑relative offset.\n\nNo `unsafe`, no FFI, no `unimplemented!()` left. All 29 tests pass."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":937,"cache_read_input_tokens":33014,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":937,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4f934af5-987f-4609-ba51-5b2a2ac345a1","uuid":"6fdff71a-4612-4456-8fe4-a9d1adab8ce8"}
